ot hide them. It puts priorities, dependencies and the decisions still to be made in the same place, so people can respond to a shared picture.</p><ul><li>Separate essential needs from preferences.</li><li>Explain what each key space is intended to support.</li><li>Record assumptions that still need to be tested.</li><li>Identify who needs to be part of the next decision.</li></ul><h2>Keep the brief useful.</h2><p>A brief is a working document, not a ceremonial first step. Revisit it when comparing options. Ask whether a proposed direction supports the original priorities, and make changes explicit when those priorities evolve.</p><p>That clarity gives the design process a firmer starting point—and gives everyone a better way to discuss what comes next.</p><div class="forme-article-end"><NuxtLink :to="`${base}/insights`" class="forme-link"><svg class="rw-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 12H4m7-7-7 7 7 7" /></svg> Back to the journal</NuxtLink><NuxtLink :to="`${base}/studio`" class="forme-link">Edit this sample’s title <svg class="rw-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 18 18 6M6 6h12v12" /></svg></NuxtLink></div></div></article>
